## C3 — freshness: every `behind` relation row the engine register holds carries a graded recheck row whose verdict the counter re-derives from committed anchor extracts

Frozen predicate (one leg, one receipt):

> `python3 tools/round83-pin-rechecks.py --repo <lane> --register registry/engine-pin-relations.tsv
> --rows registry/pin-recheck-rows.tsv` prints one `PIN_RECHECK rows=5 graded=4 holds=4 reworded=0
> moved=0 drifted=0 ungraded=1 bad=0` line and exits 0 only when every data row of the relation
> register has exactly one recheck row keyed by `(engine, ref)`; a graded row declares a verdict from
> {`holds`, `reworded`, `moved`, `drifted`}, the newest tag observed at its `date`, the pin-side and
> newest-side anchor extracts (each a committed file whose first line is
> `# url=<https-url> sha256=<64-hex> ref=<ref> lines=<a>-<b>`), a quote of at most 25 words on each
> side, and the line numbers those quotes sit on; and the counter re-derives the verdict from the two
> committed extracts — `holds` requires the pin-side quote to occur verbatim in the newest extract,
> `reworded`/`moved`/`drifted` require it to be absent there and the newest-side quote to be non-empty
> and different from it. A row is `ungraded` only when the relation names a ref the lane holds no
> document for, and it must then carry `verdict=ungraded` and a `note` naming the missing document;
> `ungraded` never counts as graded. A row whose key names no relation row, whose extract is missing or
> whose header digest disagrees with the extract bytes, whose quote exceeds 25 words, whose quote does
> not occur at its declared line, or whose verdict contradicts the byte comparison is `bad`. It exits
> 1 naming every finding and 2 when a register is absent or unreadable.

Target: `PIN_RECHECK rows=5 graded=4 holds=4 reworded=0 moved=0 drifted=0 ungraded=1 bad=0`, exit 0 —
relations 1, 2, 4 and 5 graded by `holds` from the round-82 `SK01` read (the newest llama.cpp tag is
`b12110`, which is byte-identical to `b12103`), relation 3 (`llama.cpp b7200`, the cross-lane kengaku
role) ungraded because the lane holds no document at that ref. Before: `registry/pin-recheck-rows.tsv`
does not exist — `could-not-evaluate: no registry/pin-recheck-rows.tsv`, exit 2. The four graded rows
are mechanical from the round-82 receipt; the round's own work is the register, the extracts, the
counter and the verdict re-derivation.

The relation register's live numbers are re-read this round (`python3 tools/pin-watch.py check --pins
registry/engine-pins.tsv`); a moved `newest` changes the row's `date` and tag, never its verdict rule.

Controls (committed as receipts before data):
1. **absent register**: the leg prints `could-not-evaluate: no registry/pin-recheck-rows.tsv`, exit 2.
2. **control fixture**: a register row declaring `drifted` over two byte-identical extracts must be
   `bad` (exit 1) — the byte comparison, not the declared word, decides.
3. **selftest mutants**, each red: a missing row for a relation, a duplicated row, a row for a
   nonexistent relation, `holds` where the pin quote is absent at newest, `drifted` where it is
   present, a 26-word quote, a quote that does not occur at its declared line, an extract whose header
   digest disagrees with its bytes, an `ungraded` row without a note.

Also this round (freshness correction, not a separate claim): `corpus/knowledge/pin-recheck-trigger.md`
line 5 ships an unrunnable recheck command (`--repo`; `tools/pin-watch.py` takes `--pins`) — round-82
